  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Employment and decent work mainstreamed into national development plans and programmes(Zimbabwe Working)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The country faces a formidable challenge in terms of resolving the high levels of unemployment, under-employment and endemic poverty. In the absence of an explicit focus on employment and poverty reduction as development goals, the erratic growth that has occurred, over time, has not created sustainable employment levels commensurate with the labour force growth. The currency crisis and financial sector stability in Zimbabwe is the biggest challenge for the economy right now with hyperinflation and currency changes. Zimbabwe United Nations Sustainable Development Cooperation Framework adopted in 2022 has four priority areas linked to the National Development Strategy 1 which is the government&#x2019;s blueprint for 2021-2025. Through these priority areas, the United Nations is supporting Zimbabwe&#x2019;s sustainable economic transformation, especially through macro and micro-economic policies, environmental protection, harnessing the demographic dividend, as well as the promotion of employment and economic empowerment for key populations, among them youth and women, to reduce poverty in all its forms. Zimbabwe signed its fifth generation of the DWCP which is running from 2022 to 2026 aligning to the UNSDCF. The DWCP places emphasis on three priority areas which are employment promotion, social dialogue and ILS and strengthen social and labour protection.</narrative>
  </description>
